      content:
          '**Functional Classification for Wheelchair Basketball:**\n- International Wheelchair Basketball Federation (IWBF) uses a **1.0 to 4.5 point classification system** based on functional trunk control and lower extremity function\n- **L1 ASIA A classification:** She would likely be classified as a **1.0 player** — the lowest functional class. Players with no trunk control below the level of injury, no lower extremity function, and limited seated balance.\n- **Team composition rules:** A team of 5 players on court cannot exceed 14.0 total points. Having a 1.0 player means the team can field higher-point players in other positions — she has strategic value.\n- **Functional implications for play:** 1.0 players typically play guard positions (which aligns with her basketball background). Limited rebounding due to trunk control but excellent for shooting, passing, and floor generalship.\n- **Classification appeal:** Some L1 complete injuries may have functional trunk extension that could qualify for 1.5 — this should be assessed by a certified IWBF classifier.\n\n**Injury Risk Assessment:**\n- **Shoulder injuries:** Most common injury in wheelchair basketball (30-40% of all injuries). Rotator cuff impingement, labral tears, and overuse tendinopathy from repetitive pushing, shooting, and chair skills. Her existing partial supraspinatus tear is a risk factor.\n- **Hand/wrist injuries:** Contusions, fractures from falls and wheelchair contact. Gloves and taping strategies reduce risk.\n- **Pressure injuries:** Prolonged sitting in sports chair (less cushioning than daily chair), increased shear forces during aggressive play. Requires sports-specific pressure management.\n- **Falls/tips:** Wheelchair basketball involves frequent tips and falls. She must demonstrate safe fall techniques and floor-to-chair transfers.\n- **Thermal dysregulation:** L1 level has impaired lower body sweating — risk of heat illness in intense competition. Hydration and cooling protocols essential.\n- **UTI risk:** Increased with dehydration during intense training. Catheterization schedule must be maintained during tournaments.',
