          '**Q: Her right shoulder MRI shows a partial supraspinatus tear. Does this preclude her from competitive wheelchair basketball? How do you manage this?**\n\n**A:** A partial-thickness bursal-side supraspinatus tear does NOT preclude competitive wheelchair basketball but requires a structured shoulder preservation approach:\n\n1. **Severity assessment:** A 3 mm partial-thickness tear is mild and common in manual wheelchair users. Up to 70% of chronic SCI patients have rotator cuff pathology on MRI, many asymptomatic. The key question is functional — can she play with acceptable pain?\n\n2. **Pre-sport rehabilitation:** Before increasing to competitive intensity, complete a 6-8 week shoulder rehabilitation program:\n- Rotator cuff strengthening (infraspinatus, teres minor, subscapularis) with emphasis on external rotation\n- Scapular stabilizer strengthening (lower trapezius, serratus anterior)\n- Posterior capsule stretching (sleeper stretches)\n- Correct any wheelchair propulsion biomechanics contributing to impingement (semi-circular stroke pattern rather than arc pattern)\n\n3. **Sport-specific modifications:**\n- Shooting form analysis — minimize overhead repetitions during practice\n- Push technique in sports chair — optimize mechanics to reduce subacromial loading\n- Cross-training with swimming or handcycling for cardiovascular conditioning with less impingement risk\n\n4. **Monitoring protocol:** Reassess shoulder every 3 months during first competitive season. Repeat MRI if symptoms worsen significantly. If tear progresses to full-thickness or pain limits function, surgical consultation warranted.\n\n5. **Risk acceptance:** She must understand that competitive wheelchair basketball accelerates shoulder wear. Long-term shoulder preservation is the primary concern for lifelong wheelchair users. This is a quality-of-life calculation she must make with full information.',
