          '**Q: She asks about using a power-assist wheelchair for daily use to preserve her shoulders for basketball. Is this an appropriate strategy?**\n\n**A:** This is an excellent and forward-thinking question that reflects emerging best practice in SCI shoulder preservation:\n\n**Evidence supports dual-chair approach:**\n- Daily manual wheelchair propulsion accounts for the majority of cumulative shoulder stress in SCI patients — 2,000-3,000 push strokes per day\n- Adding competitive sports on top of daily propulsion demands accelerates rotator cuff degeneration\n- Using power-assist wheels (e.g., SmartDrive, e-motion) or a separate power wheelchair for daily/community mobility reduces cumulative shoulder loading while preserving manual wheelchair skills for sport\n\n**Recommended approach:**\n- **For sport:** Continue with ultralight manual sports wheelchair (described below)\n- **For daily use:** Prescribe power-assist add-on (SmartDrive MX2 or similar) for her existing daily wheelchair. This reduces push force by 40-60% while maintaining active wheeling.\n- **For community distances:** Consider lightweight power chair or power-assist for campus mobility, grocery shopping, and community errands where the distance causes shoulder fatigue.\n\n**Insurance justification:** Frame the power-assist as medically necessary for shoulder joint preservation given objective MRI findings of rotator cuff pathology and the documented correlation between cumulative propulsion and progressive shoulder degeneration in SCI. Cite the Paralyzed Veterans of America Clinical Practice Guidelines on upper extremity preservation.\n\n**Important nuance:** This is NOT about admitting inability to use a manual chair — it is a proactive sports medicine strategy analogous to a professional baseball pitcher icing their arm between starts. Frame it this way for the patient to avoid perceived loss of independence.',
