          '**Q: During her first tournament, she develops a 2 cm x 2 cm Stage 2 pressure injury on her right ischial tuberosity. She has a critical game the next day. How do you advise?**\n\n**A:** This requires balancing competitive aspirations with skin integrity — a career-threatening injury if mismanaged:\n\n1. **Immediate assessment:** Stage 2 pressure injury (partial-thickness skin loss with exposed dermis) is painful-area adjacent and requires wound care. Assess for signs of infection, depth, and undermining. If deeper than Stage 2 or infected, she CANNOT play.\n\n2. **If clean Stage 2:** She CAN potentially play with modifications, but this is a significant risk:\n- **Sports chair cushion modification:** Add gel overlay or redistribute pressure away from the affected ischium. An ATP or seating specialist at the tournament can make rapid adjustments.\n- **Protective dressing:** Apply hydrocolloid or foam dressing (Mepilex) over the wound to reduce shear.\n- **Time limitation:** Reduce playing time — suggest to the coach she plays limited minutes with frequent substitution breaks for pressure relief.\n- **Between games:** Complete pressure relief (prone or side-lying). No sitting outside of game time.\n\n3. **Risk counseling to the patient:** "I understand this game matters to you. Here is the reality — if this wound worsens to Stage 3 or 4, you could be out for months, require surgery, and potentially end your sports career before it starts. One game is not worth that. If you play, it must be with modifications and limited minutes."\n\n4. **Post-tournament mandatory plan:** Full skin assessment by physiatrist within 48 hours. Complete offloading until healed. Evaluate sports chair seating system for causative factors. This injury suggests her sports chair cushion is inadequate — upgrade before next competition.\n\n5. **Prevention going forward:** Custom pressure-mapped cushion for sports chair. Skin checks before and after every practice and game. Education on recognizing early signs.',
