          '**Q: The adaptive sports program she attends is volunteer-run and has no medical oversight. Athletes frequently play through injuries. How do you address this systems gap?**\n\n**A:** This is a common and dangerous gap in adaptive sports programming:\n\n1. **Offer to serve as medical advisor:** Volunteer your time as a physiatrist consultant to the program. Even quarterly visits for pre-participation screening and injury assessment would significantly improve safety. Frame this as professional development and community service.\n\n2. **Develop a medical screening protocol:**\n- Pre-participation physical examination template specific to wheelchair athletes\n- Shoulder screening (rotator cuff, impingement tests) at season start and mid-season\n- Skin check protocol before and after practices\n- Emergency action plan posted at every practice/game\n- First aid kit with SCI-specific supplies (catheter kits, skin care supplies)\n\n3. **Athlete education program:** Develop a "shoulder preservation for wheelchair athletes" handout covering warning signs, self-management, and when to seek care. Create a skin inspection checklist for athletes.\n\n4. **Coach education:** Many adaptive sports coaches lack SCI-specific medical knowledge. Offer a training session covering: heat illness recognition, autonomic dysreflexia awareness, pressure injury prevention, shoulder injury warning signs, bladder management during competition.\n\n5. **Connect with regional resources:** Contact the NWBA or local Paralympic sport club for established safety protocols. Many have model policies that smaller programs can adopt.\n\n6. **Advocate for institutional support:** Approach the local hospital, medical school, or VA for sponsorship. A formal partnership provides medical credibility, liability protection, and potential funding for the program.',
