    Section(
      title: 'DOMAIN E: INTERPERSONAL AND COMMUNICATION SKILLS',
      content:
          '**Scenario:** The patient\'s parents request a private meeting without their daughter. Father says: "We were in the car behind her when the accident happened. We saw everything. We almost lost her. Now she wants to play a contact sport in a wheelchair going full speed? What if she falls and gets hurt worse? Can she become more paralyzed? We want you to tell her this is too dangerous."\n\n**Supporting Patient Autonomy While Validating Parental Fear:**\n\n**Step 1 — Acknowledge the trauma:** "I cannot imagine what it was like to witness your daughter\'s accident. That kind of trauma stays with you, and it makes complete sense that you are terrified of anything that could put her at risk again. Your fear comes from a place of deep love, and I respect that."\n\n**Step 2 — Address the medical concerns directly:**\n"Let me address your specific questions honestly:\n- Can she become \'more paralyzed\'? At her level (L1 complete), the risk of worsening her spinal cord injury from wheelchair basketball is extremely low. Her injury is complete and stable. Wheelchair basketball does not involve the types of forces that would threaten her spine.\n- Injury risks that DO exist: shoulder injuries, hand injuries, pressure sores, falls from the chair. These are real but manageable with proper equipment, training, and medical oversight. They are comparable to injury rates in able-bodied recreational sports.\n- The biggest health risks for someone with SCI are actually cardiovascular disease, depression, and social isolation — all of which are REDUCED by competitive sports participation."\n\n**Step 3 — Reframe the risk equation:**\n"I understand your instinct is to protect her. But consider this: your daughter was an elite athlete. Her identity, her confidence, her social world were built around basketball. The accident took her legs, but it did not take her competitive spirit, her court vision, or her love of the game. Wheelchair basketball gives her back a part of herself that the injury took away.\n\nThe research is clear — SCI patients who participate in adaptive sports have lower rates of depression, better cardiovascular health, greater life satisfaction, and longer life expectancy than those who do not. The safest thing for her long-term health may actually be to play."\n\n**Step 4 — Refuse to undermine her autonomy:**\n"I want to be transparent — I will not tell her this is too dangerous, because I do not believe it is. She is 24 years old, cognitively intact, and making an informed decision about her own body. My role is to make sure she competes safely — proper equipment, shoulder monitoring, skin protection, and medical oversight.\n\nBut your feelings matter too, and I think she would benefit from hearing your concerns directly. Would you be open to a family conversation where we discuss the safety plan together? I think if you see the precautions we are putting in place, it may ease some of your worry."\n\n**Step 5 — Offer ongoing parental support:**\n"Adjusting to your child\'s disability is its own journey, separate from hers. Have either of you spoken with a counselor about the accident? Many parents of SCI patients benefit from their own support. The trauma you experienced watching the accident may need its own attention."',
